<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Estonian Salvation Committee</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1918">1918</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Estonian Salvation Committee was a three-member executive body formed in February 1918 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-elders-carthage">Council of Elders</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonian-provincial-assembly">Estonian Provincial Assembly</a>, made up of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konstantin-pats">Konstantin Päts</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juri-vilms">Jüri Vilms</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konstantin-konik">Konstantin Konik</a>. On 24 February 1918, between the Bolshevik withdrawal and the German entry into Tallinn, it issued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonian-declaration-of-independence">Estonian Declaration of Independence</a>. It then formed the Estonian Provisional Government.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When the Bolsheviks seized power the Maapäev declared itself the supreme authority in November 1917 and set up a <b>Salvation Committee</b> of Konstantin Päts, Jüri Vilms and Konstantin Konik.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-estonia">Republic of Estonia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>As German troops advanced and the Bolsheviks withdrew, the <b>Salvation Committee</b> declared the Republic of Estonia on 24 February 1918; the Germans then occupied the country until November.</blockquote><a href="/state/autonomous-governorate-of-estonia">Autonomous Governorate of Estonia</a></li></ul></section>
